<p>In November 1974, the Arecibo Telescope in Puerto Rico <a href="https://news.cornell.edu/stories/1999/11/25th-anniversary-first-attempt-phone-et-0">fired a 1679-bit radio message</a> toward the globular cluster Messier 13 in the constellation Hercules. 1679 is the product of two prime numbers, suggesting (hopefully) that the bits might be arranged in a 23 × 73 grid:</p>
<figure id="attachment_74667" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-74667" style="width: 200px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-15-asked-and-answered-1.jpg" alt="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arecibo_message.svg" width="200" height="600" class="size-full wp-image-74667" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-74667" class="wp-caption-text">Image: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arecibo_message.svg">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure>
<p>This gives some basic information about our civilization: the numbers 1-10; a description of the composition and structure of DNA; an image of a man, together with information about his size and the human population; a picture of the solar system, indicating which planet had sent the message; and an image of the Arecibo telescope.</p>
<p>In August 2001, <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20240113183015/https://www.cropcircleresearch.com/articles/arecibo.html">a similar 23 × 73 grid</a> was discovered impressed in a wheat field near the Chilbolton radio telescope in Hampshire:</p>
<figure id="attachment_74668" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-74668" style="width: 200px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/2026-11-15-asked-and-answered-2.jpg" alt="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chilbolton_crop_circle.svg" width="200" height="608" class="size-full wp-image-74668" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-74668" class="wp-caption-text">Image: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chilbolton_crop_circle.svg">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure>
<p>Silicon has been added to the table of atomic numbers that make up DNA; the number of nucleotides has been changed; the population value, solar system information, and transmitter diameter have changed; and a squat creature with a bulbous head has replaced the man. The message also bears a representation of a crop circle that had appeared in the same field a year earlier.</p>
<p>Had aliens responded to our message? <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20041013062350/http://seti.org/general/ao_message_crop.html">The SETI Institute Online wrote</a>, &#8220;This is highly improbable. There is no evidence to suggest an other-than-earthly origin for these graphics.&#8221;</p>
